{-
   The futex, which is the whole of what Linux gives a user-space synchronization primitive.

   Module `Native.Thread.Linux`, and a module of its own rather than a file of `Native.Thread`
   because of what separates them: everything above this is an algorithm - a state word, a compare,
   a decision to wait - and every one of those algorithms is the same on any kernel that can block a
   thread and wake it again. This is the part that is Linux's, and a port is a sibling of this
   directory rather than an edit to anything above it.

   § What a futex is, in one paragraph

   "Fast userspace mutex", and the name describes the design rather than the operation: the *fast*
   path never enters the kernel at all. A lock word is an ordinary atomic that a thread reads,
   compares and writes with ordinary instructions, and the kernel is called only when that fails and
   the thread has to stop. `FUTEX_WAIT` is "put me to sleep on this address, unless the word has
   already changed"; `FUTEX_WAKE` is "start up to n of whoever is sleeping there".

   The `unless` is the entire point and is what nothing in user space can do for itself. Between a
   waiter reading the word and deciding to sleep, and the waiter actually sleeping, the waker may
   have changed the word and looked for someone to wake - and found nobody. The waiter would then
   sleep on a condition that has already been satisfied, forever. `FUTEX_WAIT` closes that race by
   performing the comparison **inside the kernel, under the lock that the queue for that address is
   protected by**, so a wake that arrives in the window is serialized against the enqueue.

   § Private

   Every operation here is the `PRIVATE` form, which tells the kernel that no other process shares
   the address - so it may key its queue by the address's virtual value instead of resolving the
   page's identity, which is the difference between a hash and a page-table walk. A futex over
   memory shared with another process needs the shared form, and it is deliberately not here: it is
   a different contract, `Native.atomicAt` is where such memory comes from, and neither is something
   to reach by accident.
-}
import Native

{-
   Which futex operation, as a type - the same shape the call numbers next door became.

   The call *number* is not here: `SysFutex` is one of `Native.Linux`'s, because 202 is a fact about
   this kernel on this architecture rather than a fact about futexes, and it varies in the two
   dimensions that file's name states. These are the other half - what to ask the call for - and they
   are the same on every Linux there is, which is why the two halves live apart.

   FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG (128) is folded into the first pair rather than or'd at each call, so what a
   position takes is one operation and not an operation plus a flag a caller may forget. The shared
   pair is the same two operations without it, for a word the kernel itself writes: the thread-id
   slot that `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` clears is woken by the kernel's own path, which does not take
   the private flag - so a joiner waiting on it has to be waiting the same way.
-}
data FutexOp
  = @value(0)   WaitShared
  | @value(1)   WakeShared
  | @value(128) WaitPrivate
  | @value(129) WakePrivate

fn opOf(op: FutexOp) -> USize = truncate(valueOf(op)) :: USize

{-
   The address of the word, as the kernel's argument register.

   Here rather than at each caller, and here rather than in `Thread.linux.yana` where a `wordAddress`
   used to sit: what the two casts express is that *this* interface is addressed rather than typed,
   which is a fact about the futex call and about nothing above it. An `Atomic(U32)` is exactly a
   `U32`'s bytes at a `U32`'s alignment - `computeAtomic` says so - so this moves nothing and reads
   nothing.

   The borrow is what keeps it honest: the address is the storage the caller is lending, and it is
   good for exactly as long as that borrow. Taking `'Atomic(U32)` rather than `%U32` is also what
   stops a caller reaching the word any other way - the plain load that a `%U32` invited is the bug
   `join` had.
-}
fn addressWord(word: 'Atomic(U32)) -> USize = bitcast(addressOf(word)) :: USize

{-
   The kernel's own answers, which are the negated errno and not an error flag - the convention every
   call in `Native/Linux.x64.yana` follows, and for its reason: this file knows one kernel's numbers
   and nothing about what a program wants them to mean.

   Only three can come back from a wait that was asked for correctly. `EAGAIN` means the word did not
   hold what the caller said it did, which is not a failure but the *answer* - somebody changed it
   first, so there is nothing to wait for. `EINTR` means a signal arrived. Everything else is a
   caller that passed an address it does not own or an operation the kernel does not have.
-}
pub let errAgain = 11 :: Size
pub let errInterrupted = 4 :: Size
pub let errTimedOut = 110 :: Size

{-
   `futexWait(address, expected)` - sleep on `address` unless it has stopped holding `expected`.

   Zero when the thread was woken, and a negated errno otherwise. A **spurious return is normal** and
   is not an error: a signal, a wake meant for somebody else, and a value that changed between the
   caller's read and the kernel's all come back here, so every caller of this is a loop that re-reads
   its own state rather than a call that trusts the answer.

   The comparison is against a 32-bit word whatever the caller's type is, which is what the kernel
   reads and the reason every state word in this module is a `U32`.
-}
pub fn futexWait(word: 'Atomic(U32), expected: U32) -> Size =
    syscall4(numberOf(SysFutex), addressWord(word), opOf(WaitPrivate),
             widen(expected) :: USize, 0)

-- The same, giving up after `nanoseconds`. The kernel takes a `timespec` - two words, seconds then
-- nanoseconds - which is why this takes an address rather than a number: the storage has to outlive
-- the call and belongs to the caller.
pub fn futexWaitUntil(word: 'Atomic(U32), expected: U32, timespec: %U8) -> Size =
    syscall4(numberOf(SysFutex), addressWord(word), opOf(WaitPrivate),
             widen(expected) :: USize, bitcast(timespec) :: USize)

-- `futexWake(address, count)` - start up to `count` of the threads sleeping on `address`, and answer
-- how many were actually started. Waking nobody is the ordinary case on an uncontended release and
-- costs one system call, which is why every caller of this asks whether anyone can be waiting first.
pub fn futexWake(word: 'Atomic(U32), count: Size) -> Size =
    syscall3(numberOf(SysFutex), addressWord(word), opOf(WakePrivate),
             bitcast(count) :: USize)

-- The shared pair, for the thread-id word only - see `futexWaitShared` above.
pub fn futexWaitTid(word: 'Atomic(U32), expected: U32) -> Size =
    syscall4(numberOf(SysFutex), addressWord(word), opOf(WaitShared),
             widen(expected) :: USize, 0)

pub fn futexWakeTid(word: 'Atomic(U32), count: Size) -> Size =
    syscall3(numberOf(SysFutex), addressWord(word), opOf(WakeShared),
             bitcast(count) :: USize)

{-
   The clone flags that make a **thread** rather than a process, and one more that makes it joinable.

   `CLONE_VM` shares the address space, which is what makes it a thread at all; `CLONE_FS`,
   `CLONE_FILES` and `CLONE_SYSVSEM` share the working directory, the descriptor table and the
   semaphore adjustments; `CLONE_SIGHAND` shares the signal handlers and `CLONE_THREAD` puts the new
   task in the same thread group, so it shares the process id and does not become a child to be
   waited on.

   `CLONE_PARENT_SETTID` and `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` are the two that are not about sharing, and they
   come as a pair over **one word**: the kernel writes the new thread's identifier into it before the
   thread starts, and zeroes it - waking anybody sleeping on it - after the thread has finished with
   its stack. That is what makes joining both possible and safe, and neither half works alone.

   Without the first, the word holds whatever the mapping started as. A joiner reads the zero, decides
   the thread has already ended, and gives back a stack the thread is still running on - a
   use-after-free whose visibility depends on scheduling. Without the second there is nothing to wait
   for, and a thread that published its own completion would still be executing its exit on the stack
   about to be unmapped.

   One constant and not two `or`ed together at the use, which is a wart worth knowing about rather
   than a preference: `|` separates a pattern's alternatives, so `let flags = a | b` binds `a` and
   reports `b` as an unreachable alternative - a warning, and a program that silently asked for a
   different set of flags. Nothing here is worth spelling twice, so the whole set is written once.

     0x00100 CLONE_VM        0x00800 CLONE_SIGHAND   0x100000 CLONE_PARENT_SETTID
     0x00200 CLONE_FS        0x10000 CLONE_THREAD    0x200000 CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID
     0x00400 CLONE_FILES     0x40000 CLONE_SYSVSEM
-}
pub let cloneThreadFlags = 0x350F00 :: Size
